<TopicPage slug="subjects/hospitality-tourism/foundational/global-tourism">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    International tourism is one of the world's largest movements of people and money: in 2019
    about <strong>1.46 billion international tourist arrivals</strong> were recorded worldwide —
    and in 2020, about 407 million. Before the pandemic, travel and tourism supported roughly
    one in ten jobs on the planet and about a tenth of global GDP, which is why every government
    has a tourism policy and every border closure in 2020 made front-page economic news.
    Cambridge IGCSE Travel &amp; Tourism 0471 devotes topic 2, "Global tourism", to that story:
    the <strong>scale of travel and tourism</strong> (2.1), the forces that drive demand, and the
    <strong>features of destinations and their appeal to different types of tourists</strong>
    (2.2). Behind the numbers sit the topic 1 definitions you need first: domestic, inbound,
    outbound and international tourism; leisure, business (MICE), visiting friends and
    relatives, and special-interest travel.
  </p>
  <p>
    The definitions are not trivia — they are the measuring instruments. A <strong>domestic
    tourist</strong> travels within their own country (a Mombasa resident holidaying at the
    Kenyan coast); an <strong>outbound tourist</strong> leaves their country; an
    <strong>inbound tourist</strong> is someone arriving <em>into</em> the country being counted.
    The same journey can be all three at once, from three different statistical points of view —
    which is why "international tourist arrivals" is counted at the destination and why a
    Kenyan and a German ministry quote different numbers about the same beach. And the
    <strong>purpose</strong> categories change what the tourist needs: a leisure tourist buys
    relaxation and sights; the business tourist of the <strong>MICE</strong> acronym (Meetings,
    Incentives, Conferences, Exhibitions/Events) buys rooms midweek, conference halls and
    reliable Wi-Fi; <strong>VFR</strong> travellers (visiting friends and relatives) book late,
    stay long, spend little on hotels but much in shops; <strong>special-interest</strong>
    tourists — birders, divers, pilgrims, festival-goers — will travel far out of season for a
    single experience no other place offers. One tourist, but a different product for each.
  </p>
  <p>
    Below, the scale story first — thirty years of arrivals on one curve — then what moves
    demand, then what makes a place appeal to whom.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="international tourist arrivals worldwide, 1995–2023 (UNWTO) — thirty years of growth, one cliff, one rebound" widget="plotter" config={arrivalsCfg} />

  <p>
    Read the curve the way the syllabus asks you to read demand. From 1995 to 2019, arrivals
    more than doubled — a long, steady climb powered by rising disposable income and cheaper
    transport. Then 2020: a fall of roughly 72% in a single year when the health factor
    (syllabus 2.1a: "risk of disease, pandemics") overrode everything else. The rebound to
    about 1.29 billion by 2023 shows the resilience the syllabus lists among the
    <em>characteristics of travel and tourism</em> — a product that is perishable (an empty
    2020 hotel room is gone forever) but a demand that survives shocks. The same curve also
    teaches the industry's economics: because the product is perishable, operators cut prices
    sharply to fill seats rather than fly them empty, which is why last-minute bargains exist
    and why a 72% demand collapse cannot be survived by pricing alone — capacity itself had to
    shrink.
  </p>

  <h2>What moves demand: six families of factors</h2>
  <p>
    Syllabus 2.1(a) sorts the drivers into six families, and every one is visible in the chart:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Economic</strong> — levels of disposable income and government investment; the long climb tracks a world getting richer.</li>
    <li><strong>Social</strong> — demographics, fashion and trends; the beach holiday went from novelty to expectation within two generations.</li>
    <li><strong>Technological</strong> — developments in transport and information technology: jets, jumbos, budget fares, online booking and social media.</li>
    <li><strong>Political</strong> — stability, security, terrorism and imposed limits on travel; borders closing or reopening move the line instantly.</li>
    <li><strong>Environmental</strong> — extreme weather, natural disasters, and growing concern for the environment feeding the growth of sustainable tourism.</li>
    <li><strong>Health</strong> — pandemics above all; 2020 is the case study.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The families interact, and real demand is always several of them at once. The package
    holiday needed cheap jets (technological) <em>and</em> rising wages (economic) <em>and</em>
    paid annual leave (political — largely legislation); the Mediterranean's dependence on
    summer sun (environmental — seasonal characteristics) sets a rhythm that no marketing can
    fully override; and the Arab Spring shows political and economic collapse compounding —
    arrivals to some North African destinations fell by double-digit percentages within a year
    and took years to recover. Note also that demand factors work at every scale: the same six
    families explain why one family postpones a holiday (income) and why a nation's entire
    inbound market evaporates (borders).
  </p>
  <p>
    A driver worth watching separately is the growth of <strong>sustainable tourism</strong>
    (2.1b). Changing customer attitudes, media and social media influence, the wider
    availability and promotion of sustainable products, and government policies are pushing
    demand towards travel that protects destinations rather than consuming them — which matters
    because tourism, left unmanaged, can destroy the very features that made a destination
    appealing. The same demand that fills a cove can erode it.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> tourism demand is not the same as tourism's value. A
  destination can receive fewer visitors and earn more (luxury, longer stays, higher spend per
  head) or receive millions and earn comparatively little (cruise passengers ashore for hours,
  day-trippers who spend only on coffee). "Scale" therefore has two axes — volume and value —
  and destination managers choose which one to grow.</p>

  <h2>A century and a half of cheaper distance</h2>
  <p>
    The growth curve did not happen on its own — each step change came from a transport or
    business innovation that cut the cost of distance.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="from a shilling rail ticket to the jumbo jet to the budget airline — how mass travel was engineered" widget="timeline" config={tourismTimeline} />

  <p>
    Follow the timeline's logic rather than just its dates. Thomas Cook's 1841 excursion proved
    that travel could be a <em>product</em> — priced, packaged, sold to people who had never
    left home. Raitz's 1950 package proved it could be sold at a price the average worker could
    pay; the 747 proved distance could be industrialised — hundreds of seats per flight split
    the fixed cost of the crossing so thinly that long-haul fares fell in real terms year after
    year; and the low-cost carrier model proved the airplane could be run like a bus route —
    one aircraft type, fast turnarounds, secondary airports, ancillary revenue from bags and
    seats. Each innovation moved a different constraint: Cook moved <em>organisation</em>, Raitz
    <em>price</em>, the 747 <em>distance</em>, the budget carriers <em>frequency</em>. Put them
    in sequence and you have the invention of mass tourism — and the reason a 2023 flight can
    cost less than the airport taxi.
  </p>

  <h2>What makes a destination appeal</h2>
  <p>
    Syllabus 2.2 turns from demand to supply: why this place and not that one? The listed
    features of destinations are a checklist of appeal — <strong>location; weather and seasonal
    characteristics; topographical features; accessibility; travel and transport gateways and
    hubs; infrastructure</strong> such as roads, airports and ports; <strong>built and natural
    attractions; culture</strong> (traditions, language, arts, music); expected behaviours;
    tourism organisations and providers; and sustainable provisions. Worked through a real
    case: the Maldives sells weather and topography (1,200 coral islands, year-round warmth)
    but its accessibility is deliberately curated — international flights to one hub island,
    then seaplanes and boats, which is expensive, exclusive and, by keeping visitor numbers
    manageable per island, protective. Barcelona sells culture, architecture and a Mediterranean
    climate with superb accessibility — which is exactly the combination that produced
    <em>overtourism</em>: some years over 30 million visitors to a city of 1.6 million, resident
    protests, and the city government capping licensed tourist beds and restricting cruise
    terminals. The appeal features that succeeded became the management problem that followed.
  </p>
  <p>
    The analytical skill is matching features to tourist types. <strong>Families</strong> want
    safe shallow beaches, short transfers, family rooms and medical infrastructure — hence
    all-inclusive resorts. <strong>Business and MICE travellers</strong> want gateways, hubs,
    conference infrastructure and schedule density, and care little for the view — hence city
    centres near airports dominate midweek. <strong>Ecotourists</strong> want unspoiled
    topography, biodiversity and responsible operators with credible sustainability provisions
    — and will pay a premium and accept discomfort for them. <strong>Backpackers</strong> want
    cheap access, hostel infrastructure and social culture. <strong>Cultural tourists</strong>
    follow World Heritage sites, festivals and food. A destination is not simply "good" — it is
    good <em>for</em> someone, and the same feature is an asset for one type and a deterrent
    for another (a remote island is paradise for the ecotourist, a dealbreaker for the
    conference organiser).
  </p>

  <h2>Seasonality and the moving target of appeal</h2>
  <p>
    Two structural facts shape every destination's business model. First,
    <strong>seasonality</strong>: demand driven by weather, school calendars and festivals
    concentrates visitors into peaks, and the peak defines the economics — capacity, staffing
    and prices are set for six hot weeks, while the off-season's fixed costs continue. The
    classic strategies against it are diversification (ski resorts adding mountain biking and
    festivals; beach resorts adding conferences and wellness), pricing (shoulder-season
    discounts) and events. Second, the appeal list is a <em>moving target</em>. A destination's
    features are read through the type of tourism in question — domestic, inbound, outbound,
    international, a day trip or a short break (syllabus 1.1 and 1.6 classify both the trips
    and the destinations: beach and coastal, island, resort, rural, urban) — and through what
    each tourist expects and can afford. Accessibility can be transformed within a decade by a
    new airport route; a culture can become an attraction because a film or a feed made it
    visible (Iceland's boom after its banking crisis and viral promotion is the textbook
    case: arrivals roughly quadrupled in a decade). When you analyse a destination in the exam,
    the marks come from linking a named feature to a named tourist type, and then to how the
    feature is being managed or sustained — scale, appeal and management in one chain of
    reasoning.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The economic arithmetic of destinations is worth doing once, because it explains most
    policy arguments. Tourism's money multiplies locally: a visitor's hotel payment pays wages
    (spent in local shops), suppliers (farmers, fishermen), and taxes (funding infrastructure)
    — the <strong>multiplier effect</strong>. But it <em>leaks</em> too: if the hotel is
    foreign-owned, the profits, the booking-commission and much of the food are imported, and
    the local gain shrinks — which is why "how much of each tourist dollar stays?" is the
    development question, and why community-based tourism exists as a model. The other side of
    the ledger: <strong>overtourism</strong> (Venice charging day-tripper access fees from 2024
    and banning large cruise ships from the lagoon centre; Amsterdam restricting new hotels),
    environmental stress (water use in Mallorca's golf-and-apartment model; coral damage from
    anchoring), and <strong>leakage-plus-concentration</strong> — tourism revenues often pool
    in a coastal strip while the interior stays poor. Destination management (syllabus 2.3)
    means managing all of it at once: maximising positive impacts (employment, conservation
    funding, cultural revival) while minimising the negative (congestion, seasonality's
    unemployment, cultural commodification).
  </p>
  <p>
    The open question the subject is currently living through: whether sustainable tourism is
    a niche product or the industry's next default. Aviation is roughly 2–3% of global
    energy-related CO₂ emissions, and its share grows as other sectors decarbonise; customer
    attitude surveys consistently show stated preference for sustainable options that does not
    always convert into booked options at higher prices (the "attitude–behaviour gap"). Carbon
    pricing, sustainable aviation fuel mandates and rail's revival on short European routes are
    the live policy experiments. Whichever way it resolves, the syllabus's bet is clear: the
    destinations that manage their appeal — rather than merely enjoying it — are the ones still
    appealing a generation from now.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Cambridge IGCSE Travel &amp; Tourism 0471 syllabus for 2027–2029, topics 1–2 ("Global tourism": 2.1 The scale of travel and tourism; 2.2 Features of destinations); UNWTO/UN Tourism, <em>World Tourism Barometer</em> (2024) and <em>Tourism Highlights</em>; Thomas Cook archive; Ajuntament de Barcelona and Comune di Venezia tourism management plans.</p>
